{
  "result": "At the edge of town there is a library that opens before the bakery and closes after the last bus. Its windows face a square where pigeons conduct their small, urgent negotiations. Inside, the clocks seem less interested in measuring time than in giving it somewhere quiet to rest.\n\nThe librarian knows which floorboards complain and which chairs receive the best afternoon light. She does not know every visitor\u2019s name, but she remembers their habits: the boy who reads atlases backward, the woman who borrows gardening books in winter, the man who comes only to finish the crossword.\n\nOne rainy Tuesday, the power goes out. Computers darken, the heating falls silent, and for a moment everyone looks up, startled by the sudden absence of humming. Then someone laughs. The librarian finds a box of candles reserved for emergencies, though she wisely leaves them unlit. Instead, she opens the curtains.\n\nThe room becomes a patchwork of gray daylight and shadow. A child asks whether stories still work without electricity. \u201cBetter than ever,\u201d says his grandfather, and begins reading aloud.\n\nSoon other voices join him. They are not synchronized; a pirate shouts over a recipe, and a poem wanders into a description of Saturn. Nobody minds. Outside, rain polishes the square.\n\nBy evening, the lights return, but several people remain where they are, reluctant to break the spell. The boy closes his atlas and looks around, as if he has discovered a country that was never printed on any of its maps.",
